00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:06.110 align:start position:0%
dark mouse is a top-down action RPG by  indie developer Daniel Wright it takes a  lot of direct inspiration from Dark  Souls but it also builds upon the genre  in interesting ways and right now you  might be put off by that simple  aesthetic and so was I at first but  after 16 hours with this game I'm really  glad I stuck with it because dark mouse  does a lot with very little take that  visual aesthetic for example right the  color scheme is mainly made up of tan  black and Gray's which really doesn't  allow for a lot of detail to be  expressed but dark mouse decides to use  this to its advantage dark and light  become a crucial theme and the way that  line of sight is illuminated around  corners adds a haunting limbo esque  flavor to the game and this is a  developer who understands that gameplay  has to come first so the lighting and  the illumination of darkness has  gameplay implications occasionally  allowing you a sliver of vision into an  adjacent room or revealing enemies that  are lying in ambush meaning that you  have to be ready to fight at a moment's  notice  speaking of which let's fight against  this red eye knight who is revealed to  be resting around the corner in a cave  here he's one of your Mouse's  doppelgangers found throughout the world  and he mimics the weaponry and the  gameplay style that you've built up  throughout the game we both fight with a  spiked shield a curved fire imbued sword  and pyromancy spells and he quickly  overpowers me the difficulty is no joke  it's easily the most challenging game  that I've played all year the way you  control difficulty is through the ghosts  that rise alongside you at the bonfire  when you die these shades will follow  the path you last took when you died  fighting everything in their way and  disappearing when they die or when they  reach the point where you last died  meaning that every time you encounter  something in dark Mouse you have to do  it alone  the first time  but then when you die to it you get  these companions who fight alongside you  it's dark mouse's major contribution to  the souls genre and I was surprised that  I liked it as much as I did  fighting with your ghosts felt like  fighting with summoned help in Dark  Souls and you can even turn it off if  it's making things too easy but the  thing is it doesn't exactly make things  easy sometimes this ghost mechanic  simply puts you on even footing with the  really really difficult parts of the  game together me and my ghosts corner  the red-eye night we're whittling away  at his health until I am the only one  who remains a Dodger every attack I set  up for the final blow and I die yeah  it's tough honestly I think some parts  of the difficulty even need to be toned  down it's that tough for example towards  the end of the game there's this  gauntlet where you fight a multitude of  enemy mice these were so difficult that  I absolutely needed my shades to fight  alongside me which was a good example of  when the game felt almost too hard  because I was actually dying so I could  get shades to help me and this felt  mandatory needing to die to get help is  a pretty shitty situation but that said  I did go most of the game without  feeling this way so the most part the  ghost mechanic works pretty well but  let's take a step back and getting too  far ahead  you have your standard Souls control  scheme you have your light attacks your  heavy attacks your block and your dodge  shield in your left hand sword pyromancy  or bow in your right and access to an  inventory where you heal upgrade weapons  and equipment are is in the top-left  but the stamina bar is actually the  light bar in the center of the screen  which I like it highlights just how  important stamina management is in this  game the soul style combat is translated  really well to top-down rewarding  well-timed blocks and parries and  punishing overly aggressive reckless  play one of the coolest additions is the  abilities screen where you can regularly  in  best talent points that unlock new moves  and it's clever because if you had all  of these unlocked moves from the very  start then it would be way too  complicated and confusing as it stands  though you can use this ability screen  to unlock abilities related to the  situation you're in so if you're getting  shot at with arrows you can deflect  those arrows with the appropriate  ability if you're fighting against these  little spiders that keep their distance  and only attack you when your back is  turned then you can get the dash attack  and run at them and kill them  before they can back away with the  ability system you actively feel your  character getting more powerful and I  like it as you progress through the game  you find marrow which is used to invest  in your stats at the bonfire health  stamina weight capacity resilience to  stun locking and dexterity to increase  your attack speed these are all stats  that you can invest in but an odd choice  is the ability to level up your  proficiency with certain weapon classes  here which I suppose is an easier to  understand version of the strength and  dexterity system of Dark Souls but the  drawback here is that it has the  unwanted side effect of forcing a player  to choose one class of weapon that  they'll have to stick with so this  coupled with the lack of variety of  weapons in the early game will likely  frustrate a lot of players as they  invest in the wrong weapons or they'll  end up wishing that they could switch  those stats they invested in later on if  you do want to play this game I've left  a link in the description to a short  written guide to the weapons and  mechanics which I recommend you check  out if you're having trouble or you  don't want to go in totally blind  it stops you making those investment  mistakes combat like Souls largely  revolves around learning enemy patterns  and  predicting their attacks most enemies  have these patterns of behavior which  means that once you figure them out  they're much easier to take out some of  the most fun I had is in these rooms  full of ranged enemies we're taking out  the enemies in the right order almost  felt like a sort of puzzle not to  mention all the traps in some levels  this one castle is like sense fortress  on steroids so dark mouse felt a lot  like hotline Miami if you've played that  game where you find yourself mapping out  the best way to deal with certain rooms  full of enemies and traps and then you  would execute that strategy you would do  it over and over tweaking it slightly  each time until you found the perfect  way to make your way through a room I'd  sort of lost that feeling in souls to be  honest but I enjoyed it in dark mouse  and it's actually very very difficult to  run past everything and just rest at the  next checkpoint which is a common  problem in souls games most enemies in  dark Mouse can match or even exceed the  speed that you run which really really  makes it difficult to run past stuff not  to mention they follow you a ridiculous  distance and you can't rest it bonfires  until you've killed them all so this  results in you often being really low on  health and creeping forward slowly just  hoping to see a bonfire enter the edge  of your screen bonfires are placed  really close together in this game but I  assume that's partly to do with the  difficulty and also because your ability  to heal in dark mouse is quite limited  random enemies drop morsels which act as  your healing and you'll have a good  store of them in the early game but by  the late game a morsel is like finding a  divine blessing in Dark Souls you only  want to use them when you really really  need to and in the version I played you  couldn't even heal in combat but the  developers always posting on the stained  page and he says that now there's a  designated button for healing which is  great but because morsels drops so  infrequently your health is a premium  that you want to preserve at all  and because health is so important this  inevitably leads to you letting your  ghosts do as much of the fighting as  possible when you get to a really tough  part and generally this was only a  problem in the late game where I started  to have a really hard time and I started  to rely on my ghost so a lot more but  thing is you don't want to ever really  be relying on your ghosts to do all the  fighting for you that feels really bad  so in the future I'd kind of like to see  more plentiful healing along with  slightly weaker ghosts so it just  increases that level of player agency  that you have moving on you definitely  don't feel alone in dark mouse even  though it's single player  there are to escort quests I'm aware of  there's one s called quest I'm really a  big fan of the character has her traits  reinforced by her reactions to enemies  and she has this story that really  drives the narrative of the game and you  start to care about this character more  because she's helping you and characters  that help you in terms of game player  characters you'll end up empathizing  more with I think that makes sense right  the other escort had a tendency to get  lost behind me and kept dying to traps  plus he required me to invest a morsel a  bit of my healing in him to keep him  going so I was getting punished pretty  hard for this guy's bad reaction to all  the traps so I guess you could argue  that this was in keeping with his  character since he was meant to be a  weak warrior that you're dragging along  with you but I ended up getting pretty  frustrated with him no matter how in  keeping with his character that is but  yeah you never feel alone  ghosts fill the rooms that you're having  trouble with and at random intervals  this happens  a powerful hunter will appear rush you  down and kill you then feed on your  marrow if you die to him your maximum  health is halved and it will slowly come  back over time now in a game this hard  this is actually terrifying from  software have been trying to figure out  how to make invaders scary for a while  now in Dark Souls 2 for example they had  them invade at random intervals but dark  mouse actually gets it right by making  invaders have the opportunity to reduce  your health by half when they find you  and successfully hunt you down you  actually are pretty scared of having  half your health reduced so I do find it  pretty funny that this indie game got  that right  they make hunters and invaders pretty  scary but back to what I said at the  start this game does a lot with a little  the music is really on point and a lot  of the ambient music was even great  enough for me to put in this video for  example the boss tracks though that's  the thing I love the most  a lot of the boss music actually matches  the movement of the boss which makes the  tracks really memorable a one thing I  also loved was that the boss music  persists after you die to the boss and I  think this says that the game developer  really knows that players will throw  themselves at a boss over and over again  it's great the thumping beat of the  music keeps you hyped and it continually  reminds you of what your goal is and  which boss you're facing and it's much  more engaging I liked it it's all about  those little things dark mouse even  users can resume Stu great effect  narrowing your camera in tight sections  and expanding it when you're exploring a  vast plane exploration is a big heart of  darkness to you hug the walls and  eventually you'll find little optional  paths that hide treasures like marrow  and weapons and amulets also you have a  torch which restricts your ability to  explore new areas if it runs out however  my never really ran out unless I was  writing this script and I'd pause  the game so a lot of the time exploring  felt like an early zelda game in the  sense that you're constantly pulling out  your map and figuring out where you are  and which rooms you haven't explored yet  I loved that but I really wish my map  would stop deleting the old areas that I  had already explored it made exploring  one Castle a huge pain in the ass  because rooms I had already explored  would go dark on my map in closing I do  think that dark mouse is a really great  achievement as an indie game it brings  out some of the best elements that Dark  Souls pioneered and it even pioneers  some new mechanics of its own as time  goes on I realized that's what's going  to make a Souls like game stand out from  the rest it's coming up with a new  mechanic like the ghost system and  inserting it and seeing how it really  changes the souls mechanics and seeing  how that souls experience can be  emphasized and brought down different  ways and I think that dark mouse nails  it it still needs polish in some areas  and you'll get frustrated at some points  when you play it but it's easily worth  the $10 price and it's easily worth  sticking it through and getting through  the frustrating bits because there are a  lot of great moments in this game plus  this is actually the first souls like  game I've covered that's actually  available for purchase so go get it if  you're interested and bear in mind that  if I was more critical in this video  it's because the game is out and your  money is on the line and this is more of  a review than a preview so if you're  currently craving Dark Souls 3 I reckon  this is the best thing to tide you over  until then and I also did a little  patreon playthrough for this game so if  that sort of thing interests you you can  become a $5 patron and see that stuff in  the future so yeah regardless thank you  for watching and I'll see you in the  next one okay
 

